    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d587">

                <pb facs="0926" n="900" sameAs="frus:frus1952-54v16/pg900"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">Conference files, lot 60 D 627. CF 308</note>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">The United States Delegation to the Department of
                        State</hi></head>
                <opener>
                    <seg rendition="#float-left"><hi rendition="#small-caps">top
                            secret<lb/>priority</hi></seg>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Geneva</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1954-05-23T14:00:00+01:00">May 23, 1954—2 p.m.</date></dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>Dulte 100. Eyes only Secretary. Your Tedul 107.<anchor n="1" xml:id="d587-f1"/> I
                    will advise Eden in accordance with your instructions and provide him with a
                    confirming memorandum.<anchor n="2" xml:id="d587-f2"/> Will omit paragraph four,
                    since Australia and New Zealand have been informed and are participating and our
                    relationship to ANZUS is well understood.</p>
                <p>With regard to final paragraph of Tedul 107, it is completely impossible for me
                    to understand the reasoning which prompts the decision by our Joint Chiefs of
                    Staff that they should downgrade the rank of our representation on these five
                    power staff conferences. Regardless of the actual outcome, the simple fact that
                    very senior military officers of the five powers were meeting in Washington
                    would convince the Russians and Chinese, who inevitably would know about it,
                    that, regardless of protestations or statements to the contrary, we really
                    intended serious business. I really regret this decision more than I can
                        say.<anchor n="3" xml:id="d587-f3"/></p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps">Smith</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
    <standOff>
        <listAnnotation>
            <note corresp="#d587-f1" n="1" xml:id="d587-f1-content">
                <p>Dated May 22, <ref target="#pg888">p. 888</ref>.</p>
            </note>
            <note corresp="#d587-f2" n="2" xml:id="d587-f2-content">
                <p>In telegram Dulte 117, May 26, Smith reported that “Eden has forwarded my
                    memorandum to London and is confident it will be well received. He hopes talks
                    will start as quickly as possible and so do I. Can we not get on with them?”
                    (790.5/5–2554)</p>
            </note>
            <note corresp="#d587-f3" n="3" xml:id="d587-f3-content">
                <p>In telegram Tedul 112, May 24, the Secretary of State informed Under Secretary
                    Smith that he had “discussed final paragraph [of telegram Dulte 100] with
                    Radford and Chiefs will reconsider. Their motivation has been primarily
                    political, feeling that British wanted high ranking to obviate carrying on
                    broader political talks.” (Conference files, lot 60 D 627, CF 242)</p>
            </note>
        </listAnnotation>
    </standOff>
